// Local build flavour: an installer, and nothing for the update feed.
//
// The main config sets `createUpdaterArtifacts: true`, which is right for a real release and is
// pinned by scripts/verify-update-safety.mjs. Its cost is that `tauri build` then insists on
// TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY and stops with "A public key has been found, but no private key" -- so
// the ordinary act of putting a fixed build on the shop's own machine required the release signing
// key, which CLAUDE.md says is never to be used for one.
//
// Merged over the main config by `npm run build:windows:local` (`tauri build --config`), this turns off
// only the *update feed artifacts*: the .zip and its .sig. The NSIS installer is byte-for-byte the
// same build, and the app it installs still carries the updater pubkey, so it still checks for and
// accepts real signed releases later. Nothing about the shipped app changes -- only what is left
// in the bundle folder for a feed nobody is publishing to from here.
//
// Publishing a real update is a different command -- `npm run build:windows`, which the release
// workflow runs -- and it does not use this file, so it still requires the key. That split is the
// point: the everyday build cannot quietly stop producing update artifacts for a real release.
{
  bundle: {
    createUpdaterArtifacts: false,
  },
}
